{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-108751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2+y-x}{x-y}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x-y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2-(x-y)}{x-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2-z}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "adf80fbb6e9b2983eee210fddf39c2de7c34673c0e5684915c4ac8de47b7544c"} {"question" "q-108751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}"] ["math" "\\frac{2-z}{z}"] ["math" "\\frac{2z-3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{2z}{3(z+1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{z}{z+4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={tan}^{2}⁡(4x+4y).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={tan}^{2}⁡(4(x+y)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & ={tan}^{2}⁡(4z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}={tan}^{2}⁡(4z)+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)={tan}^{2}⁡(4z)+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "adf80fbb6e9b2983eee210fddf39c2de7c34673c0e5684915c4ac8de47b7544c"} {"question" "q-108744" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108744" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{tan}^{2}⁡(4z)"] ["math" "{tan}^{2}⁡(4z)+1"] ["math" "{tan}^{2}⁡(4z)-4"] ["math" "{tan}^{2}⁡z+4"] ["math" "{tan}^{2}⁡z-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108744" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{tan}^{2}⁡(4z)+1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}+6xy+3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3(x+y)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3{z}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=3{z}^{2}+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "adf80fbb6e9b2983eee210fddf39c2de7c34673c0e5684915c4ac8de47b7544c"} {"question" "q-108736" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108736" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{z}^{2}"] ["math" "3{z}^{2}+1"] ["math" "3{z}^{2}-2"] ["math" "6{z}^{2}+2"] ["math" "6{z}^{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108736" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{z}^{2}+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4x+4y-1.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4(x+y)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =4z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=4z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=4z.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "adf80fbb6e9b2983eee210fddf39c2de7c34673c0e5684915c4ac8de47b7544c"} {"question" "q-108778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2z"] ["math" "3-4z"] ["math" "4z"] ["math" "4z+3"] ["math" "4z-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4z"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108748" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{8x-12y+1}{12x-18y+3}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=2x-3y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108748" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4(2x-3y)+1}{6(2x-3y)+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-3y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-3\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}(2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{4z+1}{6z+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2z+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{1}{2z+1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108748" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "adf80fbb6e9b2983eee210fddf39c2de7c34673c0e5684915c4ac8de47b7544c"} {"question" "q-108748" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108748" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-2z"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2z+1}"] ["math" "\\frac{4z+1}{6z+3}"] ["math" "\\frac{z+1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{z+1}{z+3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108748" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2z+1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{9x-3y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=y+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3(3x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=3x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{9z-2}{3z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3z}{9z-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{3z}{9z-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\int (1+\\frac{2}{9z-2})\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(z+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|9z-2|) & =x+K \\\\ z+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|9z-2| & =3x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-y+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|27x-9y-2| & =3x+C \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|27x-9y-2| & =y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=27x-9y-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "adf80fbb6e9b2983eee210fddf39c2de7c34673c0e5684915c4ac8de47b7544c"} {"question" "q-108857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "27x-9y+3"] ["math" "27x-9y-2"] ["math" "3x-y"] ["math" "3x-y+1"] ["math" "6x-2y-3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "27x-9y-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+5y)-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=10x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5(x+y))-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5z)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5z).\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5z)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡(5z)\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5z)| & =2x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5z)| & =10x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y)|=10x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "adf80fbb6e9b2983eee210fddf39c2de7c34673c0e5684915c4ac8de47b7544c"} {"question" "q-108819" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108819" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+5y)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x+5y)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x+5y)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(5x+5y)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108819" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x+y)^{2}+2(x+y)+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(g(x,y))=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(x+y+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =(z+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=(z+1)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(z+1)^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(z+1)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z+1) & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y+1)=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+y+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "adf80fbb6e9b2983eee210fddf39c2de7c34673c0e5684915c4ac8de47b7544c"} {"question" "q-108775" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108775" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+y)^{2}-1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x+y+1}"] ["math" "x+y"] ["math" "x+y+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-108775" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+y+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-107815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x+y}{3-2x-y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=-\\frac{x+y}{3}+C\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-107815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x+y}{3-(2x+y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\frac{z}{3-z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6-z}{3-z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{3-z}{6-z})\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int (\\frac{z-3}{z-6})\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{3}{z-6}+1)\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|z-6|+z & =x+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+y-6|+2x+y & =x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+y-6| & =-\\frac{x+y}{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{K}{3}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=2x+y-6.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-107815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "adf80fbb6e9b2983eee210fddf39c2de7c34673c0e5684915c4ac8de47b7544c"} {"question" "q-107815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-107815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-x-y+3"] ["math" "2x+y+3"] ["math" "2x+y-3"] ["math" "2x+y-6"] ["math" "x+y-6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-107815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x+y-6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-149756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\sqrt{4x-4y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-149756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+2\\sqrt{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1+2\\sqrt{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2\\sqrt{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ -\\sqrt{z} & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{x-y}=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=-\\sqrt{x-y}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-149756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "adf80fbb6e9b2983eee210fddf39c2de7c34673c0e5684915c4ac8de47b7544c"} {"question" "q-149756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-149756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}}"] ["math" "-\\sqrt{x-y}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-y}}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{x-y}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{x+y}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "q-149756" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\sqrt{x-y}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/state.json" "file_sha256" "f72ed318c897182762dbcb408efb5cef6ff674178815d8af15c3b70c820133cc"} {"question" "e-9036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2xy - x^2 - y^2.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z= x - y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z'(x) = f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/example-9036.json" "file_sha256" "070c513fa53dee76ef1476fa793e5c632de232d251d426092c8458dbc05fa6ad"} {"question" "e-9036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-(x-y)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x - y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  1 - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  =  z^2 + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z) = z^2 + 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/example-9036.json" "file_sha256" "070c513fa53dee76ef1476fa793e5c632de232d251d426092c8458dbc05fa6ad"} {"question" "e-9038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\dfrac{1 - x - y}{x + y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = 2x + C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/example-9038.json" "file_sha256" "c78d63974f233391f1bbb6703592528d0afff09e258284580f5307ce528438bd"} {"question" "e-9038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-(x+y)}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{1-z}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int z\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{2} & =x+K \\\\ {z}^{2} & =2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = 2K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 = 2x + C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y) = (x+y)^2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14053065/example-9038.json" "file_sha256" "c78d63974f233391f1bbb6703592528d0afff09e258284580f5307ce528438bd"}] "usage" {"e-9038" [] "e-9036" []} "basis" 1789 "retractions" [[17592186175754 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2xy - x^2 - y^2$.\nUsing a substitution of the form $z= x - y$, transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z'(x) = f(z)$. What is the function $f(z)$?"] [17592186175754 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= - (x - y)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n$z = x - y$.\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - \\frac{dz}{dx} &= -z^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form\n\n$$\nz'(x) = f(z)\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = z^2 + 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(z) = z^2 + 1\n$$"] [17592186175755 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1 - x - y}{x + y}$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n$g(x,y) = 2x + C$,\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)$?"] [17592186175755 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (1 - (x + y))/(x + y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} - 1 &= \\frac{1 - z}{z}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form\n\n$$\nz'(x) = f(z)\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{z}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz\\frac{dz}{dx} &= 1 \\\\\n∫zdz &= ∫dx \\\\\n\\frac{z^{2}}{2} &= x + K \\\\\nz^{2} &= 2x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = 2K$.\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 = 2x + C\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = (x+y)^2\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
